r and `body-liveness` are reimplemented in Rust from their\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1056:stated rules. `live-exact-rule` means the Rust node recomputes every\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1057:live set of every image it admits; a received live set is never trusted.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1138:      yin-scalar     B1 scalar encoder/decoder, class declaration\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1139:      yin-image      tuples, decode, validator, body-liveness, R,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1168:    | Ph | Deliverable                   | Depends on                     |\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1187:deliverable, and it is simpler than T0 planned: the oracle is R4 itself,\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1203:file per live activation on the tail fixtures; atomicity fixtures pass;\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1204:no panic; a fault-injection test (a deliberately corrupted decoded\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1211:`:live` included; a payload parked by\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1213:same normalized outcome; `continuation-defect` refusals equal by rule.\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1247:3. K-D3. Return frames and payloads are sparse (live registers only),\ndocs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1298:with live-set write-back; guarded specialization and deoptimization;\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md | sed -n '425,450p;480,524p;1207,1215p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc | sed -n '150,210p;280,300p;345,362p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc | sed -n '193,227p;315,344p;402,430p;600,619p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects_test.cljc | sed -n '335,368p'; rg -n 'attach.*return|return.*attach|frame.*attach|call.*flight|site-pc|:live' test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_r4_test.cljc test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md | sed -n '425,450p;480,524p;1207,1215p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc | sed -n '150,210p;280,300p;345,362p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc | sed -n '193,227p;315,344p;402,430p;600,619p'; nl -ba test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects_test.cljc | sed -n '335,368p'; rg -n 'attach.*return|return.*attach|frame.*attach|call.*flight|site-pc|:live' test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_r4_test.cljc test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_test.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"   425\t`Value` constant has a non-atomic count, so one shared across threads\n   426\twould race on every `const`; revision 1 put `Vec<Value>` in the image\n   427\tand that is corrected here. Each machine materializes its own `consts`\n   428\tfrom `image.data` once, at `load-image` and at `attach-image` (appending\n   429\tonly the new image's entries, so existing indices are stable). A\n   430\t`const` instruction then clones a machine-local `Value`. The pool is\n   431\tderived state: it is rebuilt from the image on restore and never\n   432\tserialized.\n   433\t\n   434\tIndex width. Register indices, register counts, pcs, depths and\n   435\tpositions are `u32` throughout. The Clojure validator bounds a register\n   436\tindex only by its body's declared count and puts no ceiling on that\n   437\tcount (`nonneg-int?`, `debruijn_register_code.cljc:432-446`,\n   438\t`:489-494`); revision 1's `u16` would have refused or mis-decoded a\n   439\tvalid image. An image with any integer operand above `u32::MAX`, or a\n   440\tdeclared register count above the host's configured activation limit,\n   441\tis refused at load, before hashing is trusted and before execution,\n   442\twith a qualified `:host-limit` diagnostic naming the operand. That is a\n   443\tdeclared host limit of the same kind as an undeclared scalar class\n   444\t(targets T-D6) and the CLJS safe-integer bound in the same validator:\n   445\tthe host says what it cannot represent and refuses, it never truncates.\n   446\tThe limits are part of the host's declaration and appear in the kit.\n   447\t\n   448\t`body_of` replaces the linear `body-of-pc` scan (`register.cljc:81-86`),\n   449\twhich the Clojure kernel runs on every closure call and every return\n   450\t(`:327`, `:592`). The index is derived state, rebuilt by `attach-image`\n   480\t\n   481\t### 4.4 Continuation\n   482\t\n   483\t    struct ReturnFrame {                // one per non-tail closure call\n   484\t        site_pc:   u32,\n   485\t        return_pc: u32,                 // absolute; survives every attach\n   486\t        frames:    Frames,              // caller's lexical frames\n   487\t        regs:      Box<[(u32, Value)]>, // live registers only, ascending\n   488\t        dest:      u32,                 // caller's rd\n   489\t        store_of:  Option<Value>,       // caller's module store address\n   490\t    }\n   491\t    type K = Vec<ReturnFrame>;          // innermost last\n   492\t\n   493\tThis is the sparse frame the code pushes (`register.cljc:602-613`), not\n   494\tthe whole-file save sketched in targets 3.2. Saving only `live` is\n   495\trequired, not optional (register design 5.2.2: a parked actor loop must\n   496\tnot retain dead messages). A return frame carries no code space (r7,\n   497\t`register.cljc:315-321`): `return_pc` is absolute in the machine's code\n   498\tspace.\n   499\t\n   500\t`:live` and the wire form. The Clojure frame carries `:live`\n   501\t(`register.cljc:611`) and `return-frame-defect` checks it against the\n   502\tcall instruction (`debruijn_register_effects.cljc:190-194`). The Rust\n   503\tframe's SERIALIZED form carries `:live` too, with the same key and the\n   504\tsame bytes: parity is defined over the canonical encoding, and K2\n   505\tasserts it frame by frame. The in-memory struct does not hold a second\n   506\tcopy, because for an admitted frame `:live` is a function of two things\n   507\tthe frame already has: it equals the `live` operand of the non-tail\n   508\t`call` at `site_pc` (the rule the validator enforces) and equals the\n   509\tindex column of `regs` (`:continuation-registers`). The wire module\n   510\twrites `:live` from `regs`' indices; on decode, `continuation-defect`\n   511\truns over the received data, `:live` included, before any struct is\n   512\tbuilt, so a frame whose `:live` disagrees is refused with\n   513\t`:continuation-live` exactly as on Clojure. Storing it again in memory\n   514\twould be a persisted copy of a derived fact with a consistency\n   515\tobligation and no reader.\n   516\t\n   517\tReturn rebuilds the caller's file at the body's declared size, fills the\n   518\tsaved pairs, writes the value to `dest` (`register.cljc:322-344`).\n   519\t\n   520\t### 4.5 The machine\n   521\t\n   522\tEvery field of the Clojure state has exactly one home. Fields are\n   523\tgrouped by owner; the grouping is the ownership boundary of 2.2.\n   524\t\n  1207\tmachine on the same node runs to completion.\n  1208\t\n  1209\tK2 completion: for every effect fixture, the Rust kernel's payload and\n  1210\teffect descriptor equal R4's in canonical encoding, every return frame's\n  1211\t`:live` included; a payload parked by\n  1212\tRust restores under R4 and one parked by R4 restores under Rust, to the\n  1213\tsame normalized outcome; `continuation-defect` refusals equal by rule.\n  1214\t\n  1215\tK3 is a gate in the sense targets gave T4: numbers the owner reads\n   150\t                      (= 2 (count pair))\n   151\t                      (nonneg-int? (nth pair 0))))\n   152\t               regs)))\n   153\t\n   154\t\n   155\t(defn- return-frame-defect\n   156\t  \"The first defect of one return `frame` of a payload whose code space\n   157\t   is `segment`. A frame carries no code space of its own\n   158\t   (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r7): its absolute pcs are checked\n   159\t   against the payload's.\"\n   160\t  [segment frame frame-idx]\n   161\t  (if-not (map? frame)\n   162\t    {:rule :continuation-continuation, :frame-index frame-idx}\n   163\t    (let [{:keys [site-pc return-pc frames regs live dest]} frame]\n   164\t      (or (when (not= format-tag (:format frame format-tag))\n   165\t            {:rule :continuation-format, :frame-index frame-idx})\n   166\t          (let [insts (:instructions segment)\n   167\t                len (count insts)]\n   168\t            (or (when-not (and (nonneg-int? site-pc) (< site-pc len))\n   169\t                  {:rule :continuation-pc, :frame-index frame-idx})\n   170\t                (let [inst (nth insts site-pc)\n   171\t                      op (first inst)]\n   172\t                  (or (when-not (= :call op)\n   173\t                        {:rule :continuation-pc, :frame-index frame-idx})\n   174\t                      (when (true? (nth inst 4))\n   175\t                        {:rule :continuation-pc, :frame-index frame-idx})\n   176\t                      (let [body-idx (body-of-pc segment site-pc)\n   177\t                            body (nth (:bodies segment) body-idx)\n   178\t                            call-rd (nth inst 1)\n   179\t                            call-live (nth inst 5)]\n   180\t                        (or (when-not (and (= (inc site-pc) return-pc)\n   181\t                                           (= body-idx\n   182\t                                              (body-of-pc segment return-pc)))\n   183\t                              {:rule :continuation-pc, :frame-index frame-idx})\n   184\t                            (when-not (= call-rd dest)\n   185\t                              {:rule :continuation-destination,\n   186\t                               :frame-index frame-idx})\n   187\t                            (when-not (< dest (:registers body))\n   188\t                              {:rule :continuation-destination,\n   189\t                               :frame-index frame-idx})\n   190\t                            (when-not (= call-live live)\n   191\t                              {:rule :continuation-live,\n   192\t                               :expected call-live,\n   193\t                               :actual live,\n   194\t                               :frame-index frame-idx})\n   195\t                            (when-not (valid-regs-shape? regs)\n   196\t                              {:rule :continuation-registers,\n   197\t                               :frame-index frame-idx})\n   198\t                            (when-not (= live (mapv (fn [p] (nth p 0)) regs))\n   199\t                              {:rule :continuation-registers,\n   200\t                               :frame-index frame-idx})\n   201\t                            (when-not (every? (fn [p]\n   202\t                                                (vm/machine-data? (nth p 1)))\n   203\t                                              regs)\n   204\t                              {:rule :continuation-registers,\n   205\t                               :frame-index frame-idx})\n   206\t                            (when-not (and (vector? frames)\n   207\t                                           (every? vector? frames)\n   208\t                                           (vm/machine-data? frames))\n   209\t                              {:rule :continuation-frames,\n   210\t                               :frame-index frame-idx})))))))))))\n   280\t(defn continuation-defect\n   281\t  \"Return nil when `payload` is a valid sparse register continuation payload,\n   282\t   or the first deterministic defect map.\n   283\t   Does not throw on malformed input.\"\n   284\t  [payload]\n   285\t  (if-not (map? payload)\n   286\t    {:rule :continuation-shape}\n   287\t    (or (when (not= format-tag (:format payload))\n   288\t          {:rule :continuation-format})\n   289\t        (when-let [d (code-space-defect (:segment payload)\n   290\t                                        (:images payload))]\n   291\t          (assoc d :rule :continuation-segment))\n   292\t        (let [expected-h (rcode/register-hash (:segment payload))]\n   293\t          (when (not= expected-h (:hash payload))\n   294\t            {:rule :continuation-hash,\n   295\t             :expected expected-h,\n   296\t             :actual (:hash payload)}))\n   297\t        (let [{:keys [segment site-pc pc resume-mode live regs dest frames\n   298\t                      continuation]} payload\n   299\t              insts (:instructions segment)\n   300\t              len (count insts)]\n   345\t                          (when-not (and (vector? frames)\n   346\t                                         (every? vector? frames)\n   347\t                                         (vm/machine-data? frames))\n   348\t                            {:rule :continuation-frames})\n   349\t                          (when-not (vector? continuation)\n   350\t                            {:rule :continuation-continuation})\n   351\t                          (some (fn [[idx f]]\n   352\t                                  (return-frame-defect segment f idx))\n   353\t                                (map-indexed vector continuation)))))))))))\n   354\t\n   355\t\n   356\t;; =============================================================================\n   357\t;; 4. Wait-entry defect validation (design section 5.2.3)\n   358\t;; =============================================================================\n   359\t\n   360\t(def ^:private stale-keys\n   361\t  [:value :status :cursor :resource-updates :stream\n   362\t   :datom :type :id :request-sent :op])\n   193\t(defn attach-image\n   194\t  \"Extend the code space of `vm` with `image`, non-destructively\n   195\t   (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, act 3): `image` is admitted alone as\n   196\t   `load-image` admits it, relocated by the held instruction count, and\n   197\t   appended, `:bodies` shifted likewise; one `[identity offset length]`\n   198\t   row, identity R of `image`, is appended to the offset table; `:hash`\n   199\t   becomes R of the concatenation. `:pc`, `:registers`, `:frames`,\n   200\t   `:continuation`, and every other register are unchanged: appending\n   201\t   moves no instruction already held. An image whose identity is already\n   202\t   a row is not attached twice, and an empty image, which adds no code,\n   203\t   is not attached at all.\"\n   204\t  [vm image contract]\n   205\t  (when-not (empty-segment? image)\n   206\t    (vm/check-contract! vm/register-contract contract)\n   207\t    (when-let [defect (rcode/register-image-defect image)]\n   208\t      (throw (ex-info (str \"Invalid register image: \" (:rule defect))\n   209\t                      defect))))\n   210\t  (let [ident (when-not (empty-segment? image) (rcode/register-hash image))]\n   211\t    (if (or (nil? ident) (some #(= ident (nth % 0)) (:images vm)))\n   212\t      vm\n   213\t      (let [held (:segment vm)\n   214\t            offset (count (:instructions held))\n   215\t            shift-body #(-> % (update :start + offset) (update :end + offset))\n   216\t            combined {:bodies (into (:bodies held)\n   217\t                                    (map shift-body)\n   218\t                                    (:bodies image)),\n   219\t                      :instructions (into (:instructions held)\n   220\t                                          (map #(relocate offset %))\n   221\t                                          (:instructions image))}]\n   222\t        (assoc vm\n   223\t               :segment combined\n   224\t               :hash (rcode/register-hash combined)\n   225\t               :images (conj (:images vm)\n   226\t                             [ident offset\n   227\t                              (count (:instructions image))]))))))\n   315\t(defn- return-transition\n   316\t  \"Unified return transition: restores caller frame from continuation if\n   317\t   pending, otherwise halts the VM with `val`. Only pc, frames, registers,\n   318\t   and continuation are restored (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3, r7): a\n   319\t   frame carries no code space, so an image attached while the call was\n   320\t   in flight stays attached, and the absolute `:return-pc` still names\n   321\t   the caller's instruction in the grown code space.\"\n   322\t  [vm val]\n   323\t  (let [continuation (:continuation vm)]\n   324\t    (if (seq continuation)\n   325\t      (let [frame (peek continuation)\n   326\t            return-pc (:return-pc frame)\n   327\t            body (body-of-pc (:segment vm) return-pc)\n   328\t            reg-count (:registers body)\n   329\t            dest (:dest frame)\n   330\t            restored-regs (reduce (fn [acc [r v]] (assoc acc r v))\n   331\t                                  (vec (repeat reg-count nil))\n   332\t                                  (:regs frame))\n   333\t            final-regs (if (some? dest)\n   334\t                         (assoc restored-regs dest val)\n   335\t                         restored-regs)]\n   336\t        (assoc vm\n   337\t               :pc return-pc\n   338\t               :frames (:frames frame)\n   339\t               :registers final-regs\n   340\t               :continuation (pop continuation)\n   341\t               :store-of (:store-of frame)\n   342\t               :value val\n   343\t               :halted? false))\n   344\t      (assoc vm :halted? true, :value val, :store-of nil))))\n   402\t(defn register-restore\n   403\t  \"The register VM's restore function, `base entry val -> state`\n   404\t   (design section 5.2.3). Validates format identity and that the entry's\n   405\t   `:image` is a row of the offset table (yin.vm.linker.md section 7.3,\n   406\t   r6; `:hash` is never a restore key, since it changes at every\n   407\t   `attach-image`), checks that the payload is defect-free via\n   408\t   effects/continuation-defect, checks that `val` is plain data, handles\n   409\t   the FFI two-step, and delivers `val` via `:write-result` or\n   410\t   `:return-result`. The entry restores registers only and never assigns\n   411\t   `:segment`: the code space is kernel state.\"\n   412\t  [base entry val]\n   413\t  (check-format! base entry)\n   414\t  (when-let [defect (effects/continuation-defect entry)]\n   415\t    (throw (ex-info \"Corrupt or tampered continuation payload\"\n   416\t                    defect)))\n   417\t  (when-not (vm/machine-data? val)\n   418\t    (throw (ex-info \"Resume value must be plain data\"\n   419\t                    {:rule :resume-value, :val val})))\n   420\t  (if (:request-sent entry)\n   421\t    (-> base\n   422\t        (update :wait-set (fnil conj [])\n   423\t                (ffi/response-wait-entry entry (:call-id entry)))\n   424\t        (assoc :value :yin/blocked\n   425\t               :blocked? true\n   426\t               :halted? false))\n   427\t    (let [call-id (:call-id entry)\n   428\t          base (if call-id (update base :parked dissoc call-id) base)\n   429\t          val (if call-id (ffi/call-result val call-id) val)]\n   430\t      (write-back base entry val))))\n   600\t                ;; The frame carries no code space (r7): its absolute\n   601\t                ;; :return-pc survives every later append.\n   602\t                continuation'\n   603\t                (if tail?\n   604\t                  continuation\n   605\t                  (conj continuation\n   606\t                        (cond-> {:site-pc pc,\n   607\t                                 :return-pc (inc pc),\n   608\t                                 :frames frames,\n   609\t                                 :regs (mapv (fn [r] [r (nth registers r)])\n   610\t                                             live),\n   611\t                                 :live live,\n   612\t                                 :dest rd}\n   613\t