{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-03 10:40:30 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-03 17:40:30 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n**P2-1: Missing `live` on `ReturnFrame` | Dismissed; architect’s optimization is safe | Evidence:** The revised design preserves wire `:live` and validates received data before constructing the struct (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:500–515`). The validator establishes both `call-live == live` and `live == indices(regs)` for every nested return frame (`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects.cljc:190–200,351–353`). Local frame construction saves exactly those indices (`src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn/register.cljc:606–611`), and return consumes `regs`, without reading `live` (`:325–335`). Image attachment preserves existing instructions and absolute pcs (`:193–203`). Thus omission loses no information or validation boundary; retaining a second copy provides no additional protection against malformed wire input.\n\n**Remaining action:** No design correction required. During K2, explicitly test nested frames with missing/mismatched wire `:live` and mismatched `regs`, confirming refusal before normalization, plus decode–encode parity after image attachment. Existing nested-frame mutation tests cover destination and malformed-register errors (`test/yin/vm/debruijn_register_effects_test.cljc:340–365`); the design already requires frame-level parity (`docs/design/yin.vm.rust-kernel.md:1209–1213`).\n\n**Ready to commit:** Yes, for this disputed design change. Implementation remains subject to the specified conformance gates."}}
